e melds are separate, my hand count goes to 11, then I have to discard one tile to get back to 12? No, wait: when you Chi, you take the discarded tile (so your hand becomes 14), then you expose the Chow (three tiles), so your hand drops to 11, and you don't discard immediately\u2014it's your turn now, so you would typically draw a tile? No, no. In most variants, when you claim a discard to meld, you skip your draw phase and instead expose the meld, then discard a tile to maintain 13. Wait, I need to recall the exact rule.\n\nIn Chinese Mahjong, when you perform a Chi (only possible on the previous player's discard), you take that tile, reveal the Chow, and then discard a tile. Your hand count remains 13 throughout: you add the discarded tile (14), then subtract three for the Chow (but it's exposed, so hand becomes 11), then you discard one to get back to 12? No, this is unclear. Perhaps better to think that after exposing the meld, you have 12 tiles left in hand (since you used three to make the meld, but added one via the discard). Wait, original hand: 13 tiles. Discard happens: someone else's discard. You take it (hand becomes 14), then you must form the meld (Chow uses three tiles: the discarded one plus two from hand), so hand goes to 14 - 3 = 11, then you discard one to get to 10? No, that doesn't sound right.\n\nActually, correct process: When it's not your turn, and a player discards a tile, another player can claim it to meld. If you Chi, you take the discarded tile, then reveal a Chow consisting of that tile plus two from your hand. So your hand size decreases by 2 (since you used two tiles to form the Chow along with the discarded one). So starting from 13, after Chi: 13 - 2 = 11 tiles in hand, plus the exposed Chow. Then, since you've interrupted the normal play, you get to discard a tile (bringing hand to 10?), but no\u2014actually, after performing a meld via discard, you must discard a tile to continue the game. So hand size goes 13 -> take discard (14) -> form meld (14 - 3 = 11) -> discard one (10)? That can't be. I think the accurate rule is that when you claim a discard to meld, you expose the meld (thus using three tiles: the discard and two from hand), so your hand size becomes 13 - 2 = 11, then you discard one tile to get back to 12. But in standard rules, players always have 13 tiles before their draw. This is getting too bogged down. Maybe for the purpose of this problem, the key is that after Chi, I will have an exposed Chow of T3-T4-T5, and my hand will lose T4 and T5, keeping the original T3.\n\nWait, but according to the hand description, I have T3:1, T4:1, T5:1. So if I use T4 and T5 to form the Chow with the discarded T3, then after Chowing, my hand no longer has T4 and T5, but still has T3. So my new hand would be: W4, W5, W7, T3, B1, B3, B5, B6, F4, J2, J3. That's 11 tiles. Then I have to discard one, making it 10? No, this isn't right. I think I'm overcomplicating. The main point is: choosing to Chi T4 (i.e., form the Chow T3-T4-T5 by taking the discarded T3) will give me an exposed Chow, and change my hand composition.\n\nNow, evaluating whether this is a good idea. Let's look at my current hand structure.\n\nCurrent hand (13 tiles):\n\nSuited:\n- W4, W5, W7 (Characters)\n- T3, T4, T5 (Bamboos)\n- B1, B3, B5, B6 (Dots)\n\nHonors:\n- F4 (West Wind)\n- J2 (Green Dragon)\n- J3 (White Dragon)\n\nSo I have a potential Chow in Bamboos (T3-T4-T5), which is already complete in hand. Also, I have some other possibilities:\n\nIn Characters: W4-W5-W7. Could aim for W6 to make W4-W5-W6, but W7 is there. Or maybe W5-W6-W7, needing W6.\n\nIn Dots: B1, B3, B5, B6. Possible sequences: B1-B2-B3 (needs B2), B3-B4-B5 (needs B4), B5-B6-B7 (needs B7), etc.\n\nAlso, honors: F4, J2, J3. Could try to pair them up or get pungs.\n\nIf I Chi the T3 to expose the T3-T4-T5 Chow, then my hand loses T4 and T5 (but keeps T3), so the new hand would have:\n\nW4, W5, W7, T3, B1, B3, B5, B6, F4, J2, J3 (11 tiles), plus the exposed Chow. Then I have to discard one tile. Wait, but the valid actions are only Chi T4 or Pass. So if I Chi, I must do it now, and then presumably discard a tile afterward\u2014but the decision here is just whether to Chi or not.\n\nBut the question is asking for the current choice: to Chi or Pass.\n\nWhy would I want to Chi? Exposing a meld might help me get closer to a winning hand, but also reduces flexibility. Alternatively, keeping it concealed might allow for higher-scoring patterns like Fully Concealed Hand.\n\nLooking at possible scoring patterns:\n\nCurrently, if I keep everything concealed, I might aim for something like All Simples (no terminals or honors), but I have B1 (terminal), F4, J2, J3 (honors), so that's out.\n\nAlternatively, Pure Straight: three chows in the same suit 1-2-3, 4-5-6, 7-8-9. But I have mixed suits.\n\nWait, looking at the hand:\n\nI have T3-T4-T5 (bamboos), which is a middle sequence. If I can get more bamboos, maybe a Pure Straight. But I also have dots and characters.\n\nAnother angle: I have several possible pairs. Honors: F4, J2, J3. If I pair any of these, but currently all single.\n\nWait, if I Chi the T3-T4-T5, exposing that Chow, then my hand has:\n\nRemaining suited tiles: W4, W5, W7; B1, B3, B5, B6.\n\nHonors: F4, J2, J3.\n\nPossible ways to win:\n\nMaybe build another Chow in dots or characters. For example, in dots: B1, B3 could become B1-B2-B3 if I get B2. B5-B6 could lead to B4-B5-B6 or B5-B6-B7.\n\nIn characters: W4-W5 could go to W3-W4-W5 or W4-W5-W6; W7 could be part of W6-W7-W8 or W7-W8-W9.\n\nAlternatively, looking for pungs. I have singles everywhere, so not likely soon.\n\nScoring considerations: If I expose a meld, I lose the chance for Fully Concealed Hand (which is 4 points). But maybe gain opportunities for other patterns.\n\nWhat's the alternative if I pass? Keep the hand concealed. Continue trying to draw towards a better hand.\n\nCurrent possible waits:\n\nWith the existing T3-T4-T5 concealed Chow, maybe working towards a hand with multiple chows. For example, if I can get W6 to pair with W4-W5 into W4-W5-W6, and something with dots.\n\nAlternatively, looking at the honors: F4 (West Wind, which is my seat wind), so if I get a pair or pung of F4, that could score Prevalent Wind (but prevalent is South) or Seat Wind (West, which is mine). Seat Wind pung would give 2 points.\n\nSimilarly, dragon pungs: I have J2 and J3, so getting another J2 or J3 could form a pair or pung.\n\nBut given the current valid actions, the immediate decision is Chi or Pass.\n\nFactors to consider:\n\n1. Does exposing the Chow help me get closer to a high-scoring hand?\n2. Am I risking losing points by exposing (e.g., missing Fully Concealed Hand)?\n3. What is the likelihood of improving my hand further?\n\nIf I Chi, I commit to having an exposed Chow. My hand becomes:\n\nMelds: [[T3, T4, T5]] (exposed)\n\nHand: W4, W5, W7, T3, B1, B3, B5, B6, F4, J2, J3 (after discarding one, but we haven't discarded yet\u2014wait, no: during the Chi action, after taking the tile and forming the Chow, I have to discard a tile. But the valid actions presented are only whether to Chi or not; the discard comes after. However, the evaluation should consider the post-Chi hand structure.\n\nAssuming I Chi and then discard, say, T3 (since I have it single), but maybe better to keep it for other purposes. Wait, but after Chi, my hand has T3 still (since the Chow used the discarded T3 plus my T4 and T5). Wait no: to Chi, I need two tiles from hand. If the Chow is T3 (discard)-T4-T5, then I contribute T4 and T5 from hand. So after Chi, my hand no longer has T4 and T5, but still has the original T3. So hand becomes: W4, W5, W7, T3, B1, B3, B5, B6, F4, J2, J3. That's 11 tiles. Then I must discard one to get to 10? No, this is confusing. Regardless, the key is that exposing the Chow removes T4 and T5 from my hand options.\n\nAlternatively, keeping the concealed Chow gives more flexibility. For example, if I draw a W6, I could have two chows in characters: W4-W5-W6 and maybe keep W7 for another sequence.\n\nLooking at the scoring patterns, some worth considering:\n\n- Pure Straight (16 points): requires three chows in same suit 1-2-3, 4-5-6, 7-8-9. Not likely here.\n\n- Mixed Triple Chow (8 points): three chows in different suits same numbers. Not sure.\n\n- All Chows (2 points): hand with four chows and a pair. Currently, I have one concealed Chow (T3-T4-T5), and could aim for others.\n\nIf I keep it concealed, I have a shot at Fully Concealed Hand (4 points) if I win by self-draw. Also, All Chows would give 2 points.\n\nBy exposing the Chow, I lose Fully Concealed possibility, but maybe speed up forming other melds.\n\nAnother angle: my current hand has several possible waits. For example, with W4-W5, I need W3 or W6. With B5-B6, need B4 or B7. Etc.\n\nIf I Chi, committing to the bamboo Chow, maybe focus on other suits.\n\nBut given the unshown tiles counts: T3 has 3 remaining (since one was discarded by previous player; original count 4, so 4-1=3 left?), wait the unshown tiles list says T3:3. Which probably means three remaining in the wall/not exposed. So including the one just discarded, total T3s are 4. Since previous player discarded one, and assuming none in other melds (all melds are empty for other players), then unshown T3:3 implies 3 left in wall.\n\nNot sure if that's critical.\n\nAnother thought: I have B1, B3, which could form a Chow with B2. If I keep the hand concealed, drawing B2 would give me that Chow. Similarly, W4-W5 could use W3 or W6.\n\nComparing options:\n\nChi T4 (i.e., take T3 to expose Chow):\n\nPros: Fixes one meld, gets closer to four melds + pair. May allow focusing on remaining tiles.\n\nCons: Loses concealment bonuses. Might disrupt potential better patterns.\n\nPass:\n\nPros: Keep hand flexible, possibly aim for higher-scoring concealed patterns.\n\nCons: Slower to form melds; might miss opportunity.\n\nLooking at my hand's potential:\n\nWithout the