\n\n- B5-B6-B7 (Chow) \u2013 need B7\n\n- Pair: say J2-J2\n\nBut this requires multiple draws.\n\nAlternatively:\n\n- Pair: F4-F4\n\n- And three more melds.\n\nBut hard to say.\n\nKey point: by exposing the Chow now, I lock in one meld, but lose flexibility. If the goal is to maximize points, concealed hands have advantages.\n\nFully Concealed Hand is 4 points, Self Drawn is 1 point. If I can get a few other small patterns, that might reach 8.\n\nFor example:\n\n- All Chows (2)\n\n- Fully Concealed (4)\n\n- Self Drawn (1)\n\n- Seat Wind if I have a pair of F3 (but I don't)\n\n- Dragon Pung if I get a pair of J2 or J3\n\nTotal could be 2+4+1+2=9 if I get Dragon Pung.\n\nBut currently, without knowing future draws, it's speculative.\n\nAlternatively, if I Chi, I might aim for Melded Hand (6 points), which requires all melds to be exposed and pair completed by discard. But that's lower scoring.\n\nConsidering the minimal points needed (8), let's see what's achievable.\n\nIf I keep hand concealed and win:\n\nPossibly:\n\n- All Chows (2)\n\n- Fully Concealed Hand (4)\n\n- Self Drawn (1)\n\n- One Dragon Pung (if I pair J2 or J3: 2)\n\nTotal: 2+4+1+2=9. Enough.\n\nWhereas if I expose a Chow:\n\n- All Chows (2)\n\n- Melded Hand (6) if all melds exposed\n\nBut Melded Hand requires every meld to be completed by other players' tiles, which might not be the case if I draw some melds.\n\nAlternatively, without Fully Concealed, harder to reach 8.\n\nGiven that, preserving the concealed status seems better.\n\nMoreover, the current valid action Chi would only give me one exposed Chow, but I still have a long way to go. By passing, I keep options open.\n\nAnother angle: the unshown tiles. Are there key tiles likely available?\n\nFor example, wanting B2 to complete B1-B2-B3. Unshown B2:4 (full count), so plenty left.\n\nSimilarly, W6:4 available.\n\nBut no specific info to guide.\n\nAlso, considering the previous player discarded T3. If I pass, someone else might Chi it, but since it's the previous player (to my right?), and turns go counter-clockwise, after me comes the next player, etc. Not sure if relevant.\n\nGiven all this, the safer play is to keep the hand concealed, aiming for higher-scoring patterns that require concealment. Passing maintains the possibility of Fully Concealed Hand and Self Drawn, which together with All Chows and maybe a Dragon pair could reach 8 points.\n\nWhereas Chowing now sacrifices those points for little immediate gain, since I still need multiple melds.\n\nTherefore, the best move is to Pass.", "output": "Evaluation: Considering the current hand structure with a concealed Chow in Bamboos (T3-T4-T5), passing preserves flexibility for higher-scoring concealed patterns like Fully Concealed Hand (4 points) and All Chows (2 points). Exposing the Chow via Chi would eliminate Fully Concealed potential and reduce opportunities to accumulate the required 8+ points through combinations like Self Drawn (1 point) and Dragon Pung (2 points). The hand has multiple viable waits (e.g., B2 for Dots Chow, W6 for Characters Chow), and honoring tiles (J2/J3) could pair for additional points. Chi offers minimal immediate benefit while sacrificing critical scoring avenues.  \nReason: Passing maintains concealment for essential patterns (Fully Concealed Hand + All Chows already provides 6 points), allowing room to reach 8+ points with a single additional pattern (e.g., Dragon Pung). Chi would lock in a low-value exposed meld, reducing adaptability and diminishing chances to meet the 8-point threshold.  \nAnswer: Pass"}
